  { question: 'How should subscription box brands build a campaign calendar?', answer: 'Around billing cycles and curation reveals: spoiler windows, skip deadlines, seasonal themes, and winback arcs — planned quarterly. SoleDrop-style VIP logic translates: engaged subscribers get early spoilers, general lists get theme emails after momentum builds. Max 2–4 SMS slots per month plus billing-critical reminders.' },
  { question: 'Should subscription box brands pair SMS with email?', answer: 'Essentially — SMS for spoiler alerts, skip deadlines, and billing reminders; Sequenzy email for unboxing guides, community stories, and winback arcs. Box fatigue is the enemy; channel coordination keeps anticipation high without message fatigue.' },
  { question: 'How does offer discipline apply to subscription box brands?', answer: 'Three-rung ladder: early spoilers for engaged subscribers (no offer), pause-save instead of cancel-save, winback discounts only to churned boxes with margin math. Discounting active subscribers teaches them to threaten cancellation quarterly.' },
  { question: 'How do you fight subscription fatigue?', answer: 'Vary box contents visibly, celebrate milestones (3rd/6th/12th box), offer skip/swap instead of cancel, and survey dormant subscribers before they churn. Email carries the variety story; SMS fires only genuine novelties. Fatigue is a curation problem wearing a marketing costume.' },
  { question: 'What about failed payments?', answer: 'Dunning texts outperform dunning emails for urgency — card-update reminders via SMS recover revenue email never touches. But separate dunning from promotion strictly: billing stream with operational tone, marketing stream with calendar discipline, never mixed.' },
];
